I have a complete picture. Here are my findings.

## Summary

This is a Spring PetClinic REST app using OfficeFloor "function" pipeline classes. All locality/region/postcode logic lives under the `org.springframework.samples.petclinic` package, split between two utility lookup tables in `util/`, a pipeline validation step in `rest/function/owner/`, the MapStruct `OwnerMapper`, and an escalation (exception) pair.

### 1. Logic that derives region/locality from a city

The derivation happens at response-mapping time (not stored on the entity), in `OwnerMapper`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` — the DTO mapping declares `locality` as a computed field and the helper delegates to `CityRegion.of`:
```java
@Mapping(target = "locality", expression = "java(locality(owner))")
...
/** The canonical region derived from the owner's city (see {@link CityRegion#of(String)}). */
default String locality(Owner owner) {
    return owner == null ? null : CityRegion.of(owner.getCity());
}
```

The actual derivation function is `CityRegion.of(String)` (full contents in section 2 below). `RespondWithOwner.java` is the pipeline step that emits the DTO via `ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner)`.

### 2. The city-to-region mapping table (exact contents)

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/CityRegion.java` — full file:
```java
public final class CityRegion {
    /** The region reported for any city not in {@link #CITY_REGIONS}. */
    public static final String UNKNOWN = "UNKNOWN";

    /** City -> canonical region. */
    private static final Map<String, String> CITY_REGIONS = Map.of(
            "Sydney", "NSW",
            "Melbourne", "VIC",
            "Brisbane", "QLD");

    private CityRegion() {
    }

    public static String of(String city) {
        return city == null ? UNKNOWN : CITY_REGIONS.getOrDefault(city, UNKNOWN);
    }
}
```
Mapping: `Sydney→NSW`, `Melbourne→VIC`, `Brisbane→QLD`; everything else (and null) → `UNKNOWN`.

### 3. Postcode-related code

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/RegionPostcodes.java` — region→postcode-range table + validator:
```java
public final class RegionPostcodes {
    /** Region -> inclusive {low, high} 4-digit postcode range. */
    private static final Map<String, int[]> REGION_RANGES = Map.of(
            "NSW", new int[] {2000, 2099},
            "VIC", new int[] {3000, 3099},
            "QLD", new int[] {4000, 4099});

    private RegionPostcodes() {
    }

    public static boolean allows(String region, int postcode) {
        int[] range = REGION_RANGES.get(region);
        return range == null || (postcode >= range[0] && postcode <= range[1]);
    }
}
```

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ValidatePostcode.java` — pipeline validation step (validates only when a postcode is present; 4 digits + within the city's region range; unknown-region cities accept any 4-digit code):
```java
public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request) throws InvalidPostcodeException {
    String postcode = request.getPostcode();
    if (postcode == null || postcode.isBlank()) {
        return;
    }
    if (!FOUR_DIGITS.matcher(postcode).matches()
            || !RegionPostcodes.allows(CityRegion.of(request.getCity()), Integer.parseInt(postcode))) {
        throw new InvalidPostcodeException(postcode, request.getCity());
    }
}
```
(`FOUR_DIGITS = Pattern.compile("\\d{4}")`.)

Supporting escalation classes:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/InvalidPostcodeException.java` — carries `postcode` and `city`, message `"Postcode '<pc>' is not valid for city: <city>"`, handled as 400.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/escalation/InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler.java` — sends `ResponseEntity.badRequest().body(ex.getMessage())`.
- YAML wiring: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/escalation/org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.escalation.InvalidPostcodeException.yml`.

Storage plumbing: `Owner` model has a `postcode` column (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`, `@Column(name = "postcode")`, with getter/setter); `ApplyOwner.java` copies it (`owner.setPostcode(request.getPostcode())`). Documented in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (postcode field ~line 2026, locality field ~line 2102).

Related but distinct address logic: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AddressNormalizer.java` and `NormalizeAddress.java` (street-abbreviation normalization — not region/postcode, included for completeness).

### 4. Tests

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` — full file (2 tests):
```java
/** locality-postcode: locality is resolved by the postcode range first (NSW 2000-2099,
 * VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), falling back to the city. A VIC-range postcode on an unknown city
 * therefore yields VIC, proving the postcode takes precedence. */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

    @Test
    void coreRegionFromPostcodeRange() throws Exception {
        int id = createOwnerOk(knownOwner("Sydney")); // postcode 2000 -> NSW
        getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.locality").value("NSW"));
    }

    @Test
    void functionalityPostcodePreferredOverCity() throws Exception {
        ObjectNode o = ownerNode(); // random (unknown) city
        o.put("postcode", "3000"); // VIC range
        int id = createOwnerOk(o);
        getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.locality").value("VIC"));
    }
}
```

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — pinned reference data + helpers (lines ~221-265):
```java
